USR0000091	DST0000622	5	The Hải Vân Pass (Vietnamese: Đèo Hải Vân, IPA: [dzɛ̂w haːj vən], 'ocean cloud pass'), is a approximately 21-kilometre (13 mi) long mountain pass on National Route 1 in Vietnam. It crosses a spur of the larger Annamite Range that empties into the South China Sea on the border of Đà Nẵng and Huế, near Bạch Mã National Park. Its name refers to the mist that rises from the sea, reducing visibility. Historically, the pass was a physical division between the kingdoms of Champa and Đại Việt from 1306 until the Vietnamese invasion in 1471.	2026-04-13 16:11:43.146+00	2026-04-13 16:11:43.146+00
